+      "evidence": "NCSC, ‘Mitigating malware and ransomware attacks’: ransomware can encrypt data and the recovery guidance prioritises recent offline backups (fetched 2026-09-04).",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F2",
+      "lesson": 3,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "Under GDPR, serious breaches must be reported to the Information Commissioner's Office within 72 hours.",
+      "truth": "A personal data breach must be reported only when it is likely to risk people’s rights and freedoms; the report must be made without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of awareness.",
+      "evidence": "ICO, ‘Personal data breaches: a guide’, ‘What breaches do we need to notify the ICO about?’ and ‘How much time do we have to report a breach?’ (fetched 2026-09-04).",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F3",
+      "lesson": 3,
+      "field": "flashcard_questions",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "Under GDPR, how quickly must a serious data breach be reported to the ICO? / Within 72 hours.",
+      "truth": "The 72-hour time limit is not unconditional: it applies to a notifiable breach, which is one likely to risk people’s rights and freedoms, and runs from awareness of the breach.",
+      "evidence": "ICO, ‘Personal data breaches: a guide’, ‘What breaches do we need to notify the ICO about?’ and ‘How much time do we have to report a breach?’ (fetched 2026-09-04).",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F4",
+      "lesson": 3,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "MEDIUM",
+      "claim": "It is illegal to copy, distribute or reproduce protected material without permission from the rights holder. … [These actions] can all be criminal offences.",
+      "truth": "Unauthorised copying can infringe copyright, but limited legal exceptions apply. Copyright infringement is not automatically a criminal offence.",
+      "evidence": "UK Intellectual Property Office, ‘Exceptions to copyright’, Overview and ‘Fair dealing’ sections (fetched 2026-09-04).",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F5",
+      "lesson": 4,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "Elements such as neodymium, cobalt and lithium are essential for batteries, screens and circuit boards.",
+      "truth": "Neodymium is a rare-earth element. Cobalt and lithium are not: cobalt is a d-block element and lithium is a group 1 element.",
+      "evidence": "USGS, ‘Rare Earths Statistics and Information’ defines rare earths as scandium, yttrium and the lanthanides; Royal Society of Chemistry periodic-table entries place lithium in group 1 and cobalt in the d block (fetched 2026-09-04).",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F6",
+      "lesson": 3,
+      "field": "conclusion_html",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "The key laws protecting computer users are: GDPR 2018, Data Protection Act 1998, Computer Misuse Act 1990, Communications Act 2003, Regulation of Investigatory Powers Act 2000, Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988, and Health and Safety legislation.",
+      "truth": "The supplied specification instead names ‘Regulation of investigatory powers act 2016’, but the official Act is the Regulation of Investigatory Powers Act 2000; the current UK data-protection framework is UK GDPR plus the Data Protection Act 2018. Either retain the legally accurate names and flag the specification defect, or mirror the supplied specification throughout. Recommend retaining legal accuracy and escalating the specification conflict to the curriculum owner.",
+      "evidence": "spec 1.3.5; GOV.UK, ‘Regulation of Investigatory Powers Act 2000’; GOV.UK, ‘The UK’s data protection legislation’ (fetched 2026-09-04).",
+      "verdict": "ADJUDICATE"
+    }
+  ],
